sections.text:
thumb|Cream, 1960s
Cream performed their first American concerts in New York City in 1967. Robert Stigwood, the group's manager, booked them for a Murray the K package show at the RKO Manhattan Theatre from 25 March to 2 April 1967.{{sfn|Roberty|1993|p=41}}  When it was finished, Stigwood arranged for a recording session with Ahmet Ertegun at Atlantic Studios.{{sfn|Brewer|2005|loc=DVD}}  Bruce and Brown had a number of new songs in various stages of development and entered the studio on 3 April.{{sfn|Brewer|2005|loc=DVD}}  Initially, Ertegun assigned Dowd to work with the trio.{{sfn|Schumacher|2003|pp=87–88}}  Dowd had worked with many of the biggest jazz and rhythm and blues musicians in the 1950s and 1960s.{{sfn|Schumacher|2003|pp=87–88}}  However, Cream was his first exposure to extreme volume levels.{{sfn|Schumacher|2003|p=88}}  The group arrived at Atlantic with their concert setup of multiple Marshall amplifiers (each 100 watts).{{sfn|Schumacher|2003|p=88}}{{efn|Cream's equipment was painted in psychedelic designs and colours by the Dutch design collective the Fool and was first used at the Murray the K shows.  Clapton's 1964 Gibson SG Standard (also called "the Fool"), Bruce's Fender Bass VI, and Baker's drums were painted to match.{{sfn|Oxman|2011|pp=62–66}}}}  Dowd was surprised by the amount of equipment accompanying the trio: "They recorded at ear-shattering level{{nbsp}}... Everyone I'd worked with before was using Fender Deluxes [about 20 watts] or Twins [about 80 watts]—six- and seven-piece bands that didn't play as loud as this three piece did."{{sfn|Schumacher|2003|p=88}}

Ertegun brought in producer Felix Pappalardi, who he believed could work as a go-between with the group and Dowd.{{sfn|Schumacher|2003|p=88}}  They began with "Strange Brew", "Tales of Brave Ulysses", and "Sunshine of Your Love".{{sfn|Schumacher|2003|pp=88–89}}  Ertegun previewed the demos and was unhappy, expecting blues-based material like that to be found on Fresh Cream.{{sfn|Thompson|2012|loc=eBook}} Jerry Wexler, Ertegun's Atlantic Records partner, reportedly went as far as to call it "psychedelic hogwash".  However, Booker T. Jones (producer and keyboardist of Booker T. & the M.G.'s) and Otis Redding (both whose Stax recordings at the time were distributed by Atco parent Atlantic) gave "Sunshine of Your Love" their wholehearted approval.{{sfn|Thompson|2012|loc=eBook}}  Differences were smoothed over by the time Cream returned in May 1967 to finish recording the songs for Disraeli Gears.{{efn|Pappalardi was so upset with Atlantic's reaction to Cream's early recordings, when he joined Leslie West to form Mountain, he chose to work with another record label.}}

With Pappalardi and Dowd, work continued on "Sunshine of Your Love". For his guitar solo, Clapton used a sound known as the "woman tone",{{sfn|Oxman|2011|pp=62–66}} which is described as "smooth, dark, singing, sustaining", by author Mitch Gallagher.{{sfn|Gallagher|2012|p=278}} The actual guitar that Clapton used has been identified as a 1964 Gibson SG, known as "the Fool".{{sfn|Oxman|2011|pp=62–66}}{{sfn|Welch|2000|loc=eBook}}{{efn|A caption in Robb Lawrence's 2008 book, The Early Years of the Les Paul Legacy 1915–1963 suggests that Clapton used a 1958 Gibson Les Paul Custom based on the tone: "You can hear the distinctively different tone achieved from this [Les Paul Custom] all-mahogany body in comparison to a maple-top Les Paul", although he does not compare it to the SG, which also has an all-mahogany body.}} It is one of the best-known examples of the woman tone and quotes the melody from the perennial pop standard "Blue Moon".{{sfn|Gallagher|2012|p=278}}{{sfn|Adelt|2007|p=71}} By using the song's major pentatonic scale, Clapton provides a contrast with the riff's blues scale.{{sfn|Munro|2005|p=30}}  A writer for the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame describes this as "creating a balance between the sun and the moon".

Baker plays much of the song on the tom-toms,{{sfn|Welch|2011|p=106}} described as sounding African (Schumacher){{sfn|Schumacher|2003|p=89}} and Native American (Shapiro).  Covach and Boone note he "concentrates on the lower tom sounds and uses an articulation and sound reminiscent of the jazz drumming in the Woody Herman or Benny Goodman bands".{{sfn|Covach|Boone|1997|pp=84–85}}

In 2004, the song ranked number 65 on Rolling Stone magazine's list of the "500 Greatest Songs of All Time". In March 2005, Q magazine placed "Sunshine of Your Love" at number 19 on its list of the "100 Greatest Guitar Tracks Ever!" In 2009, VH1 included it at number 44 on its list of the "Top 100 Hard Rock Songs". The song is on the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame's list of the "500 Songs That Shaped Rock and Roll".

Ertegun later admitted that, while his tastes ran more to Robert Johnson (Clapton had recorded Johnson's "Ramblin' on My Mind" with John Mayall, "Crossroads" with the Powerhouse, and "Four Until Late" with Cream), Cream's and Pappalardi's vision resulted in songs which had a much larger impact on the rock audiences of the time.{{sfn|Brewer|2005|loc=DVD}}  Covach and Boone identified "Sunshine of Your Love" as foreshadowing future trends in rock:
{{Quote|'Sunshine of Your Love', Cream's best-known song, is a culmination of the British adaptation of blues into rock and also the direct precursor of Led Zeppelin and heavy metal, where this type of blues-based motivic riff and harmonic motions like A–C–G or E–G–A (as in "Whole Lotta Love") serve as the basis for a seemingly endless number of songs.{{sfn|Covach|Boone|1997|p=85}}}}

