Several live recordings of "Sunshine of Your Love" have been issued on Cream albums.<ref name="Unterberger"/> These include a 24 October 1967 recording by the [[BBC Radio|BBC]] (''[[BBC Sessions (Cream album)|BBC Sessions]]''), 9 March 1968 at the [[Winterland Ballroom]] (''[[Live Cream Volume II]]''), and 26 November 1968 at the [[Royal Albert Hall]] (''[[Cream's Farewell Concert]]'').<ref name="Unterberger"/> A recording from Cream's reunion show on 3 May 2005 is included on ''Royal Albert Hall London May 2-3-5-6, 2005''.<ref name="Unterberger"/> During their post-Cream careers, Clapton and Bruce recorded several live performances of the song.<ref name="Unterberger"/>

A variety of musicians have recorded "Sunshine of Your Love".<ref name="Unterberger"/> After Cream announced their breakup, Hendrix often performed it in concert as a tribute to the group, apparently unaware that they had dedicated the song to him.{{sfn|Shapiro|Glebbeek|1990|pp=137, 300}}  He played it as an instrumental and sometimes as part of a medley.{{sfn|Belmo|Loveless|1998|pp=461–465}}  A performance by the [[The Jimi Hendrix Experience|Experience]] on 4 January 1969 is one of the best-known.{{sfn|McDermott|Kramer|Cox|2009|p=134}}  During the live broadcast of ''A Happening for Lulu'', a music variety show hosted by pop singer [[Lulu (singer)|Lulu]] on [[BBC Television]], the Experience suddenly broke with the programme.{{sfn|McDermott|Kramer|Cox|2009|p=134}}  Hendrix announced, "We'd like to stop playing this rubbish ["Hey Joe"] and dedicate a song to the Cream, regardless of what kind of group they may be in – dedicate this to Eric Clapton, Ginger Baker, and Jack Bruce".{{sfn|Shapiro|Glebbeek|1990|pp=326–327}}  As their performance of "Sunshine of Your Love" ran into the time allotted for Lulu's closing number, the show's producer and staff were frantically signalling for the Experience to stop.  However, they continued playing and the show ended on a fade.{{sfn|McDermott|Kramer|Cox|2009|p=134}}  Hendrix later apologised to Lulu, who thought the performance made for a great television moment.{{sfn|Black|1999|p=173}}

[[Ella Fitzgerald]] covered the song during live performances starting in the late 1960s, as captured on 1968's [[Sunshine of Your Love (album)|''Sunshine of Your Love'']] and ''Live At Montreux 1969''. A version by the Japanese metal band [[Gastunk]] appeared at number 83 on the [[Oricon]] singles chart on December 12, 1988.{{sfn|Oricon|1998|p=92}}

The band [[Living Colour]] covered the song for the 1994 film ''[[True Lies]]''.<ref>{{Cite magazine |date=1994-07-30 |title=For Soundgarden, Soul Asylum Producer Michael Beinhorn, Personality Is Paramount |magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]] |last=Bambarger |first=Bradley |page=110}}</ref> The version peaked at number 22 [[Official Aotearoa Music Charts|in New Zealand]] on its debut week ending September 25, 1994.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Living Colour – 'Sunshine of Your Love' |url=https://charts.nz/showitem.asp?interpret=Living+Colour&titel=Sunshine+Of+Your+Love&cat=s |access-date=2026-01-06 |website=charts.org.nz}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Official Top 40 Singles (23–29 September 1994) |url=https://aotearoamusiccharts.co.nz/archive/singles/1994-09-23 |access-date=2026-01-06 |website=[[Official Aotearoa Music Charts]]}}</ref>

